// @error WRITTEN THROUGH
// @error-at 30
// A9's `Writable` HALF, AND THE WRITE IS THROUGH A CALLEE'S PARAMETER (D821).
//
// `const b: Shape[] = a` is a covariant list assignment: `a` stores plain `Circle` rows and
// `b` stores union boxes. Read-only, that pair LOWERS — D791 gives `b` a converted copy,
// which nothing can tell apart from the alias. Here `poison(b)` hands the list to a body
// that STORES into it, and the two answers the language has both fail:
//
//   · an ALIAS admits `a[0]` reading back a `Sq` through a `Circle`-typed handle, which is
//     D661B's unsoundness and is what master's invalid module was standing in for;
//   · a COPY gives `b` a private list, so the store is invisible through `a` while VL's
//     lists are references everywhere else in the language.
//
// So the ASSIGNMENT is what is illegal. That is `DECISIONS.md`'s A9 ruling — `Readable` /
// `Writable` inferred during parameter inference, no annotation surface — and nothing here
// is spelled by the author.
//
// THE ANALYSIS IS PER VALUE AND FOLLOWS THE ARGUMENT EDGE. `b`'s alias set is `{a, b, xs}`:
// the passthrough binding joins it and so does `poison`'s parameter, because a write in ANY
// function through ANY alias is the write that observes the divergence. Master's rep-scoped
// scan reached this cell too; what it could not do is spare
// `list-literal-widen-unrelated-write.vl`.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
function poison(xs: Shape[]) { xs[0] = { s: 3 } }
function f() {
  const a: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const b: Shape[] = a
  poison(b)
  print(a.length)
  print(b.length)
}
f()
